BODY = '''<h2>What OrthoInfo and the CDC Say</h2>
<p>OrthoInfo, run by the American Academy of Orthopaedic Surgeons, gives two fitting rules for a cane. When you stand up straight, the top of the cane should reach the crease in your wrist, and your elbow should be slightly bent when you hold it. It also says to hold the cane in the hand opposite the side that needs support, so if your right leg is sore, hold it in your left hand.</p>
<p>For walking, OrthoInfo says to set the cane about one small stride ahead, step onto the sore leg and then finish with the good leg. Going up stairs, hold the cane in the hand opposite the sore leg, put your other hand on the handrail and step up with the good leg first. Going down, put the cane on the step first, then the sore leg, then the good leg.</p>
<p>The CDC says more than 1 in 4 older adults falls each year, that fewer than half tell their doctor, and that a single fall doubles the chance of another.</p>
<p class="note"><strong>The wrist crease is the check that fails most often.</strong> A cane set too high or too low changes how your arm and shoulder work. Stand in the shoes you wear, let your arm hang and set the height to your wrist crease, as OrthoInfo says. If you are unsure which side or which cane, ask a doctor or physical therapist.</p>

<h2>How to Choose a Cane</h2>
<h3>Height range</h3>
<p>The ranges here run from 29 to 39 inches. The DMI goes to 39, the Medline starts at 29, and the KINGGEAR and HONEYBULL stop at 36. Check that your wrist-crease height falls inside the range with room to spare.</p>
<h3>Base: single tip, quad or pivoting</h3>
<p>A single tip is light and slim. A quad or three-tipped base stands on its own and gives more ground contact, but it is wider and heavier. The KINGGEAR has four tips, the HONEYBULL three and the HurryCane a pivoting base.</p>
<h3>Handle</h3>
<p>An offset handle, like the DMI's, Medline's and KINGGEAR's, puts your hand over the shaft. A T handle, like the HurryCane's, is straight. Choose the one your wrist finds comfortable.</p>
<h3>Weight limit</h3>
<p>The limits run from 250 pounds on the DMI to 350 on the HurryCane, which Drive says it tested. The HONEYBULL listing text gives none. Choose a limit above the weight of the person using it.</p>
<h3>Folding</h3>
<p>The HurryCane, KINGGEAR and HONEYBULL fold, which helps in a bag or a car. The DMI and Medline do not.</p>

<h2>Walking Cane Questions</h2>
<h3>Which hand do I hold a cane in?</h3>
<p>OrthoInfo says to hold the cane in the hand opposite the side that needs support. If your right knee or hip hurts, hold the cane in your left hand, so the cane and the sore leg move forward together. OrthoInfo's walking steps are to set the cane about one small stride ahead, step onto the sore leg, then finish with the good leg.</p>
<h3>How tall should a cane be?</h3>
<p>OrthoInfo says the top should reach the crease in your wrist when you stand up straight, and your elbow should be slightly bent when you hold the handle. Stand in the shoes you wear and have someone measure the crease from the floor. Our cane and walker height checker compares your number with the height range of each cane in this guide.</p>
<h3>How do I use a cane on stairs?</h3>
<p>Going up, hold the handrail with one hand and the cane in the other, and step up with the good leg first. Going down, put the cane on the step first, then the sore leg, then the good leg, according to OrthoInfo.</p>
<h3>Is a quad cane better than a single-tip cane?</h3>
<p>A quad base stands on its own and gives more contact with the floor, but it is wider and heavier. A single tip is lighter. If you need more support than a cane gives, a rollator or walker may suit you better. See our <a href="/best-rollators/">rollator guide</a>.</p>
<h3>When should I replace the tip?</h3>
<p>OrthoInfo tells walker users to check the rubber tips regularly and replace them if they become uneven or worn, and the same check is sensible for a cane tip, since the tip is where the cane meets a wet or smooth floor. Replacement tips are cheap, and the DMI and Medline canes here use standard single tips. Do not use a cane with a split, hardened or worn-through tip.</p>
<h3>When is a cane not enough?</h3>
<p>A cane takes some weight off one side. If you need support on both sides, tire after a short distance or feel unsteady even with the cane, ask a doctor or physical therapist whether a walker or rollator would suit you better. A rollator adds a seat and brakes, and a standard walker gives a wider base. See our rollator guide and the article on choosing between a cane, walker and rollator.</p>

<h2>What the Research Says</h2>
<p>The large Cochrane review of exercise found that balance and functional exercise cut the rate of falls by 24% in high-certainty evidence, and it did not test canes. Use a cane for support, and ask a physical therapist about balance work too. See <a href="/articles/what-exercise-prevents-falls-cochrane-review/">the exercise evidence</a>.</p>

<h2>Our Take</h2>
<p>The HurryCane Freedom Edition is the best pick if you want a maker-backed folding cane. Drive says it is tested to 350 pounds and gives the height range and a limited lifetime warranty, and 26,168 reviews average 4.6 stars. It costs about twice as much as the plain canes, and the Amazon seller is a third party.</p>
<p>For a cane that stands on its own at a low price, the KINGGEAR quad is the one to buy, with a 300-pound rating on its listing and 17,106 reviews at 4.6. For a plain cane, the DMI is the lightest and goes up to 39 inches, and the Medline is the cheapest, with a 300-pound rating.</p>
<p>The HONEYBULL has the most reviews of any folding cane here, but find its weight limit first, because the listing text we read gives none.</p>

<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
<p>The HurryCane Freedom Edition is the best all-round cane, the KINGGEAR the best quad base, and the DMI and Medline the best plain canes. Whichever you choose, set the height to your wrist crease, hold it in the hand opposite the sore side and use the handrail on stairs, as OrthoInfo advises. See also our guides to <a href="/best-rollators/">rollators</a> and <a href="/best-shower-chairs/">shower chairs</a>.</p>'''

